<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valdemar I of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1131–1182</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Valdemar the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valdemar I, called the Great, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from 1157 to 1182, ending a long period of civil war. With his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> he led repeated campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> of the southern Baltic coast. In 1168 they captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> on Rügen, destroyed the idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a> and brought the Rani under Danish overlordship and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Valdemar I of Denmark" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author of book and creator of the drawing of the bracteate: P. Hauberg (Peter Christian Hauberg) [1844-1928] · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bracteate,_1157,_comemorating_wedding_of_king_Valdemar_(the_Great)_%26_queen_Sophia_(of_Minsk)_of_Denmark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
